{"parentUuid":"3fdf9da9-b699-4aa1-adb5-cee53693b586","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root/SimPO","sessionId":"6821caff-8cb6-4a84-bed9-ac55d7c78dbd","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"glistening-enchanting-church","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01YAamdTPZMwwk3h1UFpeP7U","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SimPO loss equation formula beta gamma average log probability Bradley-Terry 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Bradley–Terry model - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%E2%80%93Terry_model\"},{\"title\":\"SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward | Zihao Tang’s Homepage\",\"url\":\"https://ishikura-a.github.io/paper_reading/2024/06/02/SimPO.html\"},{\"title\":\"SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward | alphaXiv\",\"url\":\"https://www.alphaxiv.org/overview/2405.14734v3\"},{\"title\":\"Lecture 24 — The Bradley-Terry model\",\"url\":\"https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/stats/stats200/stats200.1172/Lecture24.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Bradley-Terry Models in R\",\"url\":\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/BradleyTerry2/vignettes/BradleyTerry.html\"},{\"title\":\"Eﬃcient Bayesian Inference for Generalized Bradley-Terry Models Fran¸cois Caron\",\"url\":\"https://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~doucet/caron_doucet_bayesianbradleyterry.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/SimPO:-Simple-Preference-Optimization-with-a-Reward-Meng-Xia/c3f1fae241a3c2449e675ab750873d800f95513c\"},{\"title\":\"Real-statistics\",\"url\":\"https://real-statistics.com/reliability/bradley-terry-model/\"},{\"title\":\"NeurIPS Poster SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward\",\"url\":\"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/96741\"},{\"title\":\"Fitting the Bradley-Terry model to large and potentially sparse datasets • BradleyTerryScalable\",\"url\":\"https://ellakaye.github.io/BradleyTerryScalable/articles/BradleyTerryScalable.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the SimPO loss equation and its key components:\n\n## SimPO Loss Equation Components\n\nThe SimPO reward function is defined as r_SimPO(x,y) = β · p_θ(y|x), where β is a constant that controls the scaling of the reward difference. The reward is calculated as the average log probability of all tokens in a response using the policy model.\n\nA target reward margin term γ>0 is introduced to the Bradley-Terry objective to ensure that the reward for the winning response r(x,y_w) exceeds the reward for the losing response r(x,y_l) by at least γ, with the preference model: p(y_w ≻ y_l|x) = σ(r(x,y_w) - r(x,y_l) - γ).\n\n### Key Features:\n\n1. **Length Normalization**: The length normalization (division by |y|) is crucial for preventing the model from exploiting response length to achieve higher rewards\n\n2. **Reference-Free Design**: This reward formulation better aligns with model generation and eliminates the need for a reference model, making it more compute and memory efficient\n\n3. **Bradley-Terry Framework**: The target reward margin is introduced to the Bradley-Terry objective to encourage a larger margin between the winning and losing responses\n\nThis was published in 2024 and represents a simpler alternative to Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) for aligning large language models with human preferences.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"88d50383-3854-41ac-be0f-675cd3211294","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:14:27.338Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"SimPO loss equation formula beta gamma average log probability Bradley-Terry 2024","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_vrtx_01V8bnTUtPYi8WEioqKk6Gso","content":[{"title":"Bradley–Terry model - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%E2%80%93Terry_model"},{"title":"SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward | Zihao Tang’s Homepage","url":"https://ishikura-a.github.io/paper_reading/2024/06/02/SimPO.html"},{"title":"SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward | alphaXiv","url":"https://www.alphaxiv.org/overview/2405.14734v3"},{"title":"Lecture 24 — The Bradley-Terry model","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/stats/stats200/stats200.1172/Lecture24.pdf"},{"title":"Bradley-Terry Models in R","url":"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/BradleyTerry2/vignettes/BradleyTerry.html"},{"title":"Eﬃcient Bayesian Inference for Generalized Bradley-Terry Models Fran¸cois Caron","url":"https://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/~doucet/caron_doucet_bayesianbradleyterry.pdf"},{"title":"[PDF] SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/SimPO:-Simple-Preference-Optimization-with-a-Reward-Meng-Xia/c3f1fae241a3c2449e675ab750873d800f95513c"},{"title":"Real-statistics","url":"https://real-statistics.com/reliability/bradley-terry-model/"},{"title":"NeurIPS Poster SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization with a Reference-Free Reward","url":"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/96741"},{"title":"Fitting the Bradley-Terry model to large and potentially sparse datasets • BradleyTerryScalable","url":"https://ellakaye.github.io/BradleyTerryScalable/articles/BradleyTerryScalable.html"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the SimPO loss equation and its key components:\n\n## SimPO Loss Equation Components\n\nThe SimPO reward function is defined as r_SimPO(x,y) = β · p_θ(y|x), where β is a constant that controls the scaling of the reward difference. The reward is calculated as the average log probability of all tokens in a response using the policy model.\n\nA target reward margin term γ>0 is introduced to the Bradley-Terry objective to ensure that the reward for the winning response r(x,y_w) exceeds the reward for the losing response r(x,y_l) by at least γ, with the preference model: p(y_w ≻ y_l|x) = σ(r(x,y_w) - r(x,y_l) - γ).\n\n### Key Features:\n\n1. **Length Normalization**: The length normalization (division by |y|) is crucial for preventing the model from exploiting response length to achieve higher rewards\n\n2. **Reference-Free Design**: This reward formulation better aligns with model generation and eliminates the need for a reference model, making it more compute and memory efficient\n\n3. **Bradley-Terry Framework**: The target reward margin is introduced to the Bradley-Terry objective to encourage a larger margin between the winning and losing responses\n\nThis was published in 2024 and represents a simpler alternative to Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) for aligning large language models with human preferences."],"durationSeconds":11.38265255},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3fdf9da9-b699-4aa1-adb5-cee53693b586"}
